 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "Their accusation «إن هذا لساحر عليم» is framed «حين بهرهم ما رأوا من الآيات… وطلبوا لها التأويلات الفاسدة»; their fear-mongering (110) as «خوفوا ضعفاء الأحلام». No figures; footsteps only for the heralds sent to the cities. STAGING v4: Same court as 7_103-108 (palace setting, Egypt, no pyramids): Musa is a blue drop labelled Musa (present: Al-Muyassar 111, those present at Musa's debate), Pharaoh a faceless king in the Egyptian headdress, his chiefs three faceless men in dark robes. Harun is named only by a pronoun (111:3) and is not drawn. The chiefs speak (109:1, 110:1, 111:1); no one points at Musa during their accusation (109:6-9, framed by the gloss). 110:6-7: the tafsirs differ on who speaks (Al-Muyassar: Pharaoh asks; As-Sa'di: they consult among themselves), so no speaker is marked, only a wide shot. 111:2 the camera on Pharaoh as they advise him; a footsteps plate for the heralds sent to the cities (111:6-111:7), drawn only once the camera is wide and gone before the close-up on Pharaoh at 112:1. No magicians are drawn yet (they arrive in 113)."
